The '''2006 Texas Longhorns football team''' represented the [[University of Texas at Austin]] in the [[2006 NCAA Division I FBS football season]]. The team's head football coach was [[Mack Brown]]. The Longhorns (also known as Texas or UT or the Horns) played their home games in [[Darrell K Royal–Texas Memorial Stadium]] (DKR), which during 2006 was undergoing some renovations to improve older sections as well as to add extra seating capacity.

The 2006 team was the defending national champions since the [[2005 Texas Longhorn football team|previous year's team]] won both the [[Big 12 Conference]] championship and the [[NCAA Division I-A national football championship|National Championship]]. That was the program's second Big 12 Championship<ref name="Big12_standings">{{cite web|url=http://www.mackbrown-texasfootball.com/doc_lib/06_records_3_big12.pdf |title=All Time Big-12 Standings |publisher=University of Texas at Austin Department of Athletics |access-date=June 26, 2006 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20060706183656/http://www.mackbrown-texasfootball.com/doc_lib/06_records_3_big12.pdf |archive-date=July 6, 2006 |url-status=dead |df=mdy }}</ref>  (27 conference championships total, including 25 in the [[Southwest Conference]]),<ref name="SWC_standings">{{cite web|url=http://www.mackbrown-texasfootball.com/doc_lib/06_records_3_swc.pdf |title=All Time SWC Standings |publisher=University of Texas at Austin Department of Athletics |access-date=June 26, 2006 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20060706183040/http://www.mackbrown-texasfootball.com/doc_lib/06_records_3_swc.pdf |archive-date=July 6, 2006 |url-status=dead |df=mdy }}</ref> and fourth consensus national championship in football.<ref name="NCAA_Championship_list">{{cite web |url=https://www.ncaa.org/champadmin/ia_football_past_champs.html|title=Past Division I-A Football National Champions|publisher=National Collegiate Athletic Association|access-date=June 27, 2006|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20060613014918/http://www.ncaa.org/champadmin/ia_football_past_champs.html|archive-date=June 13, 2006}}</ref>  Their championship victory in the [[2006 Rose Bowl]] was also the 800th win for the program and the Longhorns entered the season ranked third in the all-time list of both total wins and winning percentage (.7143).<ref name="UNT notes">{{cite web|url=http://www.mackbrown-texasfootball.com/doc_lib/fb_notes_082806.pdf |title=Texas – Game 1 |publisher=University of Texas at Austin Department of Athletics |access-date=October 22, 2006 |url-status=dead |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20070809094639/http://www.mackbrown-texasfootball.com/doc_lib/fb_notes_082806.pdf |archive-date=August 9, 2007 |df=mdy }}</ref>

In 2006, the Longhorn's game against [[Ohio State University]] in September was one of the most anticipated college football games of the [[Glossary of American football#R|regular season]].<ref name="Don't miss">{{cite web |url=https://www.espn.com/college-football/preview06/news/story?id=2555753|title=Don't miss these games in 2006|publisher=[[ESPN]]|author=Duffy, David|date=August 20, 2006 |access-date=December 13, 2006}}</ref><ref name="Feldman">{{cite web |url=http://insider.espn.go.com/espn/blog/index?name=feldman_bruce#20060726|title=September's intriguing matchups|author=Feldman, Bruce|publisher=[[ESPN]]|date=July 26, 2006|access-date=August 3, 2006|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20060805061230/http://insider.espn.go.com/espn/blog/index?name=feldman_bruce|archive-date=August 5, 2006|url-status=live}}</ref><ref name="Field_lift"/><ref name="now"/><ref name="Fort Wayne">{{cite news |url=http://www.fortwayne.com/mld/journalgazette/sports/colleges/15415713.htm?template=contentModules/printstory.jsp |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20070930195604/http://www.fortwayne.com/mld/journalgazette/sports/colleges/15415713.htm?template=contentModules%2Fprintstory.jsp |url-status=dead |archive-date=September 30, 2007 |title=Games to watch, week-by-week |newspaper=[[Fort Wayne Journal Gazette]] |author=Warden, Steve |date=September 1, 2006 |access-date=December 13, 2006 |df=mdy-all }}</ref> Texas lost the game to Ohio State and completed the regular season with an overall record of 9 wins – 3 losses, and a 6–2 record in conference games. They were ranked 19th in the [[Bowl Championship Series]] (BCS) rankings, issued prior to the [[NCAA football bowl games, 2006–07|bowl season]].<ref name="rankings 12-03">{{cite web |url=https://www.espn.com/college-football/rankings|title=2006 College Football Rankings – Week 15|publisher=[[ESPN]]|date=December 3, 2006|access-date=December 4, 2006|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20061205103114/http://sports.espn.go.com/ncf/rankingsindex|archive-date=December 5, 2006|url-status=live}}</ref>  The Longhorns ended their season with a victory in the [[2006 Alamo Bowl]] against the unranked, 6–6 [[Iowa Hawkeyes]] to improve to an overall record of 10 wins – 3 losses. They were ranked 13th in the final national rankings by both the [[Associated Press]] [[AP Poll]] and the [[Coaches Poll|USA Today Coaches Poll]]<ref name="final rankings"/> As of May 1, 2007 seven players from this team had been drafted by [[NFL|professional football teams]] and two more had signed professional contracts as [[free agent]]s.

==Leading into the 2006 season==
[[File:2006 UT football fall scrimmage.JPG|thumb|2006 Fall Orange vs. White intra-team scrimmage]]
In most preseason rankings, Texas was listed in the top five. In the initial [[USA Today]] Coaches' Poll, which is part of the [[Bowl Championship Series]] formula for determining which two teams play for the national championship, Texas was ranked number two.<ref name="coach-poll1">{{cite news |url=https://www.usatoday.com/sports/college/football/2006-08-03-top-25-capsules_x.htm|title=Breaking down the top 25 teams|newspaper=[[USA Today]]|date=August 5, 2006|access-date=August 7, 2006}}</ref>  The same poll had Ohio State number 1,<ref name="pre-season-OSU">{{cite news |url=https://www.usatoday.com/sports/college/football/2006-08-04-ohio-state-poll-cover_x.htm|title=Buckeyes are preseason favorites in USA TODAY Top 25 Coaches' poll|newspaper=[[USA Today]]|date=August 4, 2006|access-date=August 7, 2006|first=Mike|last=Dodd}}</ref> meaning that if both teams held their ranking until their September 9, 2006 match-up, it would be a rarely seen early-season meeting of the two most highly ranked teams in the sport.<ref name="1v2">{{cite magazine |author=Mandel, Stewart|url=http://sportsillustrated.cnn.com/si_blogs/football/ncaa/2006/08/get-ready-for-no-1-vs-no-2.html|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20110604162657/http://sportsillustrated.cnn.com/si_blogs/football/ncaa/2006/08/get-ready-for-no-1-vs-no-2.html|url-status=dead|archive-date=June 4, 2011|title=Get Ready for No. 1 vs No. 2|magazine=[[Sports Illustrated]]|date=August 4, 2006|access-date=August 7, 2006}}</ref> The teams did keep their respective ranking until game day and played the earliest ever meeting of the No. 1 and No. 2 ranked teams.<ref name = "earliest ever"/>

{{see also|2006 NCAA Division I-A football rankings}}

==Stadium renovations==
[[Darrell K Royal-Texas Memorial Stadium]] underwent renovations just prior to the season. US$15 million were allocated by the board of regents for stadium renovations. Stage one, which began on November 14, 2005, involved updating Bellmont Hall – which sits in the west end of the stadium – to meet newer safety codes set by the Austin Fire Department. Also included were water sealing the stadium and expansion of both the Centennial Room and eighth-floor [[press box]].  $8 million was spent on audiovisual improvements, including a {{convert|7370|sqft|m2|adj=on}} [[High-definition video|high-definition]] [[Daktronics]] [[LED]] scoreboard, nicknamed "[[Godzillatron]]". At the time of its creation, Godzillatron was the first high-definition video screen in college sports and the largest high-definition video screen in the world,<ref name="McCoy_scoreboard">{{cite news |url=http://www.dailytexanonline.com/media/storage/paper410/news/2006/09/05/Sports/Colt-Mccoy.Silences.Critics.As.Longhorns.Roll.Over.North.Texas-2256711.shtml?norewrite200609080233&sourcedomain=www.dailytexanonline.com|title=Colt McCoy silences critics as Longhorns roll over North Texas|author=Kilian, Ryan|newspaper=[[The Daily Texan]]|location=University of Texas at Austin|date=September 5, 2006|access-date=September 8, 2006}}{{dead link|date=June 2016|bot=medic}}{{cbignore|bot=medic}}</ref><ref name="scoreboard">{{cite news
|author=Maher, John|title=That's one big Bevo of a scoreboard|newspaper=[[Austin American-Statesman]]|url=http://www.statesman.com/sports/content/sports/stories/longhorns/05/10texfoot.html|access-date=May 11, 2006|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20060603132712/http://www.statesman.com/sports/content/sports/stories/longhorns/05/10texfoot.html|archive-date=June 3, 2006}}</ref> though it was quickly surpassed by a larger screen in Tokyo. It is still the largest high-definition video screen in collegiate sports.<ref name="Texas Debuts Daktronics Video Board">{{cite press release|title=Texas Longhorns Choose Daktronics for HD Video Display at DKR-Texas Memorial Stadium |publisher=[[Daktronics]] |url=http://www.daktronics.com/dak_news.cfm?articleID=527 |access-date=May 10, 2006 |url-status=dead |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20060821215356/http://www.daktronics.com/dak_news.cfm?articleID=527 |archive-date=August 21, 2006 }}</ref><ref name="measurements">{{cite web |url=https://www.engadget.com/2006/05/10/longhorns-to-snatch-worlds-biggest-hd-display-title-from-dolp/|title=Longhorns to snatch "world's biggest HD display" title from Dolphins?|work=Engadget|date=May 10, 2006|access-date=September 7, 2006|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20061014012817/http://www.engadget.com/2006/05/10/longhorns-to-snatch-worlds-biggest-hd-display-title-from-dolp/|archive-date=October 14, 2006|url-status=live}}</ref><ref name="wow">{{cite web |url=http://www.texassports.com/index.php?s=&change_well_id=2&url_article_id=4499|title=New scoreboard brings the "Wow!" factor to football stadium|author=O'Keeffe, Kevin|publisher=University of Texas at Austin Department of Athletics|date=July 1, 2006|access-date=July 13, 2006|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20070926224945/http://www.texassports.com/index.php?s=&change_well_id=2&url_article_id=4499|archive-date=September 26, 2007|url-status=dead}}</ref><ref name="scoreboard_dissapoints">{{cite news |url=http://www.dailytexanonline.com/media/storage/paper410/news/2006/09/05/TopStories/New-Screens.Ad.Coverage.Disappoints.Some.Students-2256782.shtml?norewrite200609071730&sourcedomain=www.dailytexanonline.com|title=New screen's ad coverage disappoints some students|author=Salinas, Andy|date=September 5, 2006|newspaper=[[The Daily Texan]]|location=University of Texas at Austin}}{{dead link|date=June 2016|bot=medic}}{{cbignore|bot=medic}}</ref><ref name="Godzillatron">{{cite news |url=http://www.statesman.com/sports/content/sports/stories/longhorns/09/03/3texscoreboard.html|title=Godzillatron roars to life – Fans get first look at scoreboard to end all scoreboards, still a work in progress|newspaper=[[Austin American-Statesman]]|author=Robbins, Kevin|date=September 3, 2006}}{{dead link|date=June 2016|bot=medic}}{{cbignore|bot=medic}}</ref><ref name="Ad">{{cite web|url=http://margaretsoltan.phenominet.com/2006/09/after-its-premiere-godzillatron_03.html|title=After its Premiere, Godzillatron Renamed By UT Fans: It's Now Adzillatron|author=Soltan, Margaret|date=July 3, 2006|access-date=July 13, 2006|url-status=dead|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20160110175013/http://margaretsoltan.phenominet.com/2006/09/after-its-premiere-godzillatron_03.html|archive-date=January 10, 2016|df=mdy-all}}</ref><ref name="2debut">{{cite news |title=Colt McCoy, Godzillatron make their career debuts on Saturday|url=http://www.dailytexanonline.com/media/storage/paper410/news/2006/09/05/Sports/Colt-Mccoy.Godzillatron.Make.Their.Career.Debuts.On.Saturday-2256730.shtml?norewrite200609072352&sourcedomain=www.dailytexanonline.com|author=Blair, Alex|newspaper=[[The Daily Texan]]|location=University of Texas at Austin|date=September 6, 2006|access-date=September 7, 2006}}{{dead link|date=June 2016|bot=medic}}{{cbignore|bot=medic}}</ref><ref name="we won't">{{cite news |url=http://www.statesman.com/sports/content/sports/stories/longhorns/10/06/6asksue.html|author=Haliburton, Suzanne|newspaper=[[Austin American-Statesman]]|title=Questions on TX-OU, petitioning NCAA for eligibility|date=October 5, 2006|access-date=October 7, 2006|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20061030132309/http://www.statesman.com/sports/content/sports/stories/longhorns/10/06/6asksue.html|archive-date=October 30, 2006}}</ref>

A new temporary bleacher seating section was added behind the south endzone bringing the stadium's stated capacity to 85,123 for the 2006 season. In mid-July 2006, The University of Texas announced that Memorial Stadium was completely sold out for the upcoming 2006 season. For the first time, tickets would not be sold on an individual game basis.<ref name="MBTF-DKR">{{cite web|url=http://www.mackbrown-texasfootball.com/index.php?s=&url_channel_id=37&url_subchannel_id=&url_article_id=29&change_well_id=2 |title=Darrell K Royal-Texas Memorial Stadium |publisher=University of Texas at Austin Department of Athletics |access-date=September 7, 2006 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20060905003649/http://www.mackbrown-texasfootball.com/index.php?s=&url_channel_id=37&url_subchannel_id=&url_article_id=29&change_well_id=2 |archive-date=September 5, 2006 |url-status=dead |df=mdy }}</ref>

Stage 2 of the project began just after the final home game, as the north end-zone was expanded to include luxury boxes and an upper-deck.<ref name="aggie game notebook"/>  The lower deck was rebuilt in 2007, while the upper deck was ready for the 2008 season.<ref name="aggie game notebook"/>

