The game against [[Ohio State University]] was one of the most anticipated college football games of the regular season.<ref name="Field_lift">{{cite web |url=http://www.msnbc.msn.com/id/14672526/|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20071117054645/http://www.msnbc.msn.com/id/14672526/|url-status=dead|archive-date=November 17, 2007|title=Home field will lift Texas over Ohio St. Buckeyes vs. Longhorns on Saturday very well could be Game of the Year|author=Johnston, Joey|work=[[NBC Sports]]|date=September 7, 2006|access-date=September 7, 2006}}</ref><ref name="espn">{{cite web |url=http://insider.espn.go.com/espn/blog/index?name=feldman_bruce#20060726|title=September's intriguing matchups|publisher=[[ESPN]]|date=July 26, 2006|access-date=August 3, 2006|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20060805061230/http://insider.espn.go.com/espn/blog/index?name=feldman_bruce|archive-date=August 5, 2006|url-status=live}}</ref>  The two teams had combined for 1,576 victories in 231 total years of football prior to meeting, with the Horns ranking third on the NCAA all-time victory list with 801 wins and the Buckeyes ranking sixth with 775.<ref name="OSU-game-notes">{{cite web|url=http://www.mackbrown-texasfootball.com/doc_lib/06_ohiost_notes.pdf |title=Texas vs. Ohio State Part 2 |publisher=University of Texas at Austin Department of Athletics |access-date=September 8, 2006 |url-status=dead |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20061129074349/http://www.mackbrown-texasfootball.com/doc_lib/06_ohiost_notes.pdf |archive-date=November 29, 2006 }}</ref>

Ohio State was ranked No. 1 with Texas No. 2 in both the Associated Press and the coach's polls. The September 9, 2006, game between Texas and Ohio State became a match-up of college football's No. 1 and No. 2 ranked teams for the 36th time in NCAA history.<ref name="Clash"/><ref name="Tx-OSU-Preview"/>

Ohio State is only the second top-ranked team to ever play as a visiting football team in [[Austin, Texas]].<ref name="Clash"/>  The other was [[Southern Methodist University|SMU]] when then-No. 7 Texas beat the Mustangs 23–20 on November 4, 1950.<ref name="Clash"/><ref name="Tx-OSU-Preview"/>  Ohio State has participated in three No. 1 vs. No. 2 matchups in school history and holds a record of three wins and no losses (2–0).<ref name="now"/><ref name="Clash"/>  The most recent such win for Ohio State occurred when they beat No. 2 Michigan in their annual matchup later that same season. Texas has a 4–0 record in No. 1 vs. No. 2 matchups with all four previous victories coming en route to national championship seasons.<ref name="now"/><ref name="Clash"/> The last time UT played in a No. 1 vs. No 2. regular season game was a win over the top-ranked [[Arkansas Razorbacks]] in one of the most famous games of the UT-ARK rivalry.<ref name="now"/>  The game marked the first No. 1 vs. No. 2 matchup in the regular season since 1996, when No. 2 Florida State beat No. 1 Florida 24–21.<ref name="now">{{cite web |url=http://www.msnbc.msn.com/id/14684138/|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20071114212655/http://www.msnbc.msn.com/id/14684138/|url-status=dead|archive-date=November 14, 2007|title=Texas now No. 2, preps for No. 1 Ohio St. – Saturday will be first 1–2 showdown in regular season since 1996|work=[[NBC Sports]]|date=September 6, 2006|access-date=September 8, 2006}}</ref><ref name="Clash"/><ref name="Tx-OSU-Preview"/>  The September 9 meeting was the earliest for the No. 1 and No. 2 teams to ever face each other in any college football season.<ref name="earliest ever">{{cite web |url=http://www.msnbc.msn.com/id/14783030/|archive-url=https://archive.today/20130104115013/http://www.msnbc.msn.com/id/14783030/|url-status=dead|archive-date=January 4, 2013|title=Some already look poised for huge seasons|work=[[NBC Sports]]|author=Hayes, Neil|date=September 11, 2006|access-date=November 18, 2006}}</ref>

[[File:2006 Longhorns vs OSU McCoy handoff to Charles.jpg|left|thumb|McCoy hands off to [[Jamaal Charles]] vs. Ohio State]]
On September 4, 2006, five days prior to the game, two UT players and one former UT player were arrested in Austin and charged for [[marijuana]] possession, a Class B misdemeanor.<ref name="Players suspended">{{cite news |url=http://media.www.dailytexanonline.com/media/storage/paper410/news/2006/09/06/TopStories/Players.Suspended.For.Ohio.State.Game-2258731.shtml?sourcedomain=www.dailytexanonline.com&MIIHost=media.collegepublisher.com|title=Players suspended for Ohio State game – Education students charged with marijuana possession|newspaper=[[The Daily Texan]]|location=University of Texas at Austin|date=September 6, 2006|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20070930211923/http://media.www.dailytexanonline.com/media/storage/paper410/news/2006/09/06/TopStories/Players.Suspended.For.Ohio.State.Game-2258731.shtml?sourcedomain=www.dailytexanonline.com&MIIHost=media.collegepublisher.com|archive-date=September 30, 2007|url-status=dead}}</ref>  The two current players, [[Tarell Brown]] and [[Tyrell Gatewood]], were suspended from the team and did not play in the September 9, 2006 game against the [[2006 Ohio State Buckeyes football team]].<ref name="Players suspended"/>  Brown was also charged with a Class A misdemeanor weapons violation for being in possession of a firearm without a permit.<ref name="Players suspended"/>  Tarell Brown was listed as a starting cornerback and may have covered Ohio Statewide receiver [[Ted Ginn Jr.]] in that game.<ref name="Players suspended"/>  Replacements included [[Ryan Palmer (American football)|Ryan Palmer]] and [[Brandon Foster]], neither of whom had started a college game.<ref name="Ginn-kick">{{cite news |url=http://www.statesman.com/sports/content/sports/stories/longhorns/09/07/7bohls.html|newspaper=[[Austin American-Statesman]]|title=Ohio State's Ginn could have Texas kicking itself – Speedy returner could cost Longhorns if given room to break free|author=Bohls, Kirk|date=September 9, 2006}}{{dead link|date=June 2016|bot=medic}}{{cbignore|bot=medic}}</ref> The former player, who was the owner of the handgun, is former UT linebacker Aaron Harris.<ref name="Players suspended"/>

"I think it will be one of the most exciting games in the history of this school", said Texas coach Mack Brown.<ref name="Clash">{{cite news |url=http://www.chron.com/disp/story.mpl/sports/college/4165951.html|title=Poll shift means 1 will play 2 – Ohio State, Utah get ready to clash in Austin|author=Duarte, Joseph|newspaper=[[Houston Chronicle]]|date=September 6, 2006|access-date=September 7, 2006}}</ref> He said he had listed Ohio State at the top of his ballot in the coach's poll prior to the game. Ohio State coach [[Jim Tressel]] stated in a news conference that he cast his vote for Texas as the number one school coming into the game, but it was later revealed that his ballot listed Ohio State as number one.<ref name="Hanging chad?">{{cite magazine |title=Hanging chad? – OSU coach Tressel said he voted UT No. 1, but didn't|url=http://sportsillustrated.cnn.com/2006/football/ncaa/09/06/tressell.apvote.ap/index.html|agency=[[Associated Press]]|magazine=[[Sports Illustrated]]|date=September 6, 2006|access-date=September 8, 2006}}{{dead link|date=September 2024|bot=medic}}{{cbignore|bot=medic}}</ref>

Two [[United States Senators]], Sen. [[Mike DeWine]], R-Ohio and Sen. [[Kay Bailey Hutchison]], R-Texas, placed a [[Gambling|wager]] on the game. DeWine promised to deliver his wife's homemade chocolate-covered peanut butter Buckeye candies to Hutchison if Texas won, while Hutchison promised to give DeWine some [[Blue Bell Creameries|Blue Bell]] ice cream, made in Texas, if Ohio State won.<ref name="wager">{{cite news |url=http://sportsillustrated.cnn.com/2006/football/ncaa/09/07/senators.ohiostate.texas.ap/index.html|title=Senators wager on Texas-Ohio State|date=September 7, 2006|access-date=September 7, 2006|publisher=[[CNN]]}}{{dead link|date=September 2024|bot=medic}}{{cbignore|bot=medic}}</ref>  In [[sports book]] betting in [[Las Vegas, Nevada]] the day prior to the game, Texas was favored to win the game by 2 to {{frac|2|1|2}} points.<ref name="vegas">{{cite web |url=http://www.vegas.com/gaming/sportsline_ncaaf.html|title=Sports Line – NCAA Football|work=Vegas.com|access-date=September 8, 2006|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20060908130412/http://www.vegas.com/gaming/sportsline_ncaaf.html|archive-date=September 8, 2006|url-status=live}}</ref>

Ohio State won the game 24–7. The attendance was 89,422, the most people to ever watch a football game in the state of Texas.<ref name="Ohio_State_Box">{{cite web|url=http://www.mackbrown-texasfootball.com/000_stats/06/ut2.htm|title=Ohio State vs Texas|publisher=University of Texas at Austin Department of Athletics|access-date=September 17, 2006|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20061028013740/http://www.mackbrown-texasfootball.com/000_stats/06/ut2.htm|archive-date=October 28, 2006|url-status=dead|df=mdy-all}}New attendance record for the state of Texas</ref>

Texas's loss made it unlikely that Texas would repeat as National Champions. Since the [[Bowl Championship Series]] was formed in 1998, 11 of the 16 teams were unbeaten going into the game.<ref name="on-the-line"/>  The only time the national champion has not been unbeaten during that stretch was in 2003 when [[LSU Tigers football|LSU]] and [[University of Southern California|USC]] claimed a share of the title as each finished with one loss.<ref name="Tx-OSU-Preview">{{cite web|url=http://www.msnbc.msn.com/id/14687408/ |title=Ohio St-Texas Preview |work=[[NBC Sports]] |author=Einhorn, Justin |date=September 7, 2006 |access-date=September 7, 2006 |url-status=dead |archive-url=https://archive.today/20121209085719/http://www.msnbc.msn.com/id/14687408/ |archive-date=December 9, 2012 }}</ref><ref name="on-the-line"/>  Ohio State tackle Kirk Barton said "There'll probably be two undefeated teams at the end of the road and if you're not one of them you're probably not going to be playing for the championship. So you've got to treat every game like it's the [[Super Bowl]]. You only get one opportunity."<ref name="on-the-line">{{cite web |url=http://www.msnbc.msn.com/id/14687967|archive-url=https://archive.today/20130104002140/http://www.msnbc.msn.com/id/14687967|url-status=dead|archive-date=January 4, 2013|title=Ohio State, Texas put BCS hopes on line – Nos. 1-, 2-ranked teams can't afford loss in big matchup Saturday|agency=[[Associated Press]]|work=[[NBC Sports]]|date=September 8, 2006|access-date=September 8, 2006}}</ref>

