Texas and [[Rice University|Rice]] met in 2006 for the 89th time. The series, which began in 1914, is the fourth oldest (by number of games) in Texas history.<ref name="Rice notes">{{cite web|url=http://www.mackbrown-texasfootball.com/doc_lib/fb_notes_091106.pdf |title=Texas at Rice – Game Day Notes |publisher=University of Texas at Austin Department of Athletics |access-date=September 16, 2006 |url-status=dead |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20061129074422/http://www.mackbrown-texasfootball.com/doc_lib/fb_notes_091106.pdf |archive-date=November 29, 2006 }}</ref>

Texas and Rice were once conference foes in the [[Southwest Conference]],<ref name="Texas-Almanac">{{cite web |url=https://texasalmanac.com/topics/sports/look-back-southwest-conference|title=A Look Back at the Southwest Conference|work=Texas Almanac|access-date=July 4, 2018}}</ref><ref name="HTO-SWC">{{cite web|url=http://www.tshaonline.org/handbook/online/articles/xns02 |title=Southwest Conference |work=Handbook of Texas Online |access-date=September 16, 2006 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20091218011057/http://www.tshaonline.org/handbook/online/articles/SS/xns2.html |archive-date=December 18, 2009 |url-status=live |df=mdy }}</ref> despite the usual mismatch in ability on the field (66 Texas wins vs 21 wins for Rice, with one tie).<ref name="All-time-vs">{{cite web|url=http://www.mackbrown-texasfootball.com/index.php?s=&url_channel_id=36&url_subchannel_id=&url_article_id=298&change_well_id=2 |title=All-Time Records vs. Opponents |publisher=University of Texas at Austin Department of Athletics |access-date=September 16, 2006 |url-status=dead |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20070426101811/http://www.mackbrown-texasfootball.com/index.php?s=&url_channel_id=36&url_subchannel_id=&url_article_id=298&change_well_id=2 |archive-date=April 26, 2007 |df=mdy }}</ref>  President [[John F. Kennedy]] compared the challenge of [[Project Apollo|going to the Moon]] to the challenge faced when Rice played Texas.<ref name="moon">{{cite web|url=http://webcast.rice.edu/speeches/19620912kennedy.html |title=President John F. Kennedy – The Space Effort |work=Rice Webcast Archive |access-date=September 16, 2006 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20060912083212/http://webcast.rice.edu/speeches/19620912kennedy.html |archive-date=September 12, 2006 |url-status=dead |df=mdy }}</ref>  In addition to renewing a traditional rivalry, playing Rice in a "home and away" series allows for Texas to play games in [[Houston]], Texas, a city that is an important recruiting base for UT,<ref name="Recruiting">{{cite news |title=UT signs 25 players, including three of state's top four|url=http://www.statesman.com/horns/content/sports/stories/longhorns/02/1utsignweb.html|newspaper=[[Austin American-Statesman]]|date=February 1, 2006|access-date=September 16, 2006|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20060915030044/http://www.statesman.com/horns/content/sports/stories/longhorns/02/1utsignweb.html|archive-date=September 15, 2006}}</ref> along with having a significant [[Texas Exes]] alumni population.<ref name="Houston Exes">{{cite web |url=http://www.texasexes.com/Exes_are.htm|title=The Texas Exes Houston Chapter are|publisher=Texas Exes Houston Chapter|access-date=September 16, 2006|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20080531034003/http://www.texasexes.com/Exes_are.htm|archive-date=May 31, 2008|url-status=dead}}</ref>

The game was the first time Rice offensive coordinator [[Major Applewhite]] faced his former college team, where he enjoys "cult legend" status.<ref name="Applewhite"/>  Applewhite was a quarterback for Texas from 1998 to 2001 and set 48 school records. Many of these still stand, including the longest pass play (97&nbsp;yards), most touchdown passes in a career (60), career yards (8,353), consecutive passes without an interception (156) and most yards passing in a game (473).<ref name = "Applewhite"/><ref name="UT passing stats">{{cite web|url=http://www.mackbrown-texasfootball.com/doc_lib/records_ind_passing.pdf |title=Individual Passing Records |publisher=University of Texas at Austin Department of Athletics |access-date=September 16, 2006 |url-status=dead |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20061129074442/http://www.mackbrown-texasfootball.com/doc_lib/records_ind_passing.pdf |archive-date=November 29, 2006 }}</ref>  At Rice, Applewhite has taken the team away from the "[[Wishbone formation|Wishbone]]" offense and moved them to a more modern, one-running-back formation similar to that used by Texas.<ref name="Applewhite">{{cite web |title=Rice assistant Applewhite to face former team|url=https://www.espn.com/college-football/news/story?id=2586483|publisher=[[ESPN]]|date=September 16, 2006|access-date=September 16, 2006}}</ref>

Rice won the [[coin toss]] and elected to defer to the second half, allowing Texas to start the game on offense. Texas' opening drive used 7 rushing plays (no passes) and just over 3&nbsp;minutes to drive {{convert|65|yd|m}} for a touchdown.<ref name="Rice_play-by-play">{{cite web |url=https://www.espn.com/college-football/playbyplay?confId=&gameId=262590242|title=Texas Longhorns at Rice Owls – Play by Play|publisher=[[ESPN]]|date=September 16, 2006|access-date=September 16, 2006}}</ref> Texas followed up by scoring a touchdown on its second possession and a field goal on its third possession to earn a 17–0 lead at the end of the first quarter. Texas won the game 52–7.<ref name="Rice_play-by-play"/>

