The Longhorns first played Baylor in 1901 and faced them annually during the days of the Southwest Conference. In the 96 meetings through 2006, Texas' record was 70 wins, 22 losses, and 4 ties.<ref name="rec-vs-opp"/>  This is Texas' third-longest rivalry by number of games: only Oklahoma and Texas A&M have faced Texas more often on the football field.<ref name="rec-vs-opp"/>  The morning of the game, the betting line in Las Vegas was Texas by {{frac|27|1|2}}–28 points.<ref name="vegas-Baylor">{{cite web |url=http://www.vegas.com/gaming/sportsline_ncaaf.html|title=Sports Line – NCAA Football|work=Vegas.com|access-date=October 14, 2006|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20061016033124/http://www.vegas.com/gaming/sportsline_ncaaf.html|archive-date=October 16, 2006|url-status=live}}</ref>

Baylor quarterback [[Shawn Bell]] used [[play action]] and passed for a {{convert|67|yd|m|adj=on}} touchdown on the first play.<ref name="unhloster">{{cite news |url=http://www.dailytexanonline.com/media/storage/paper410/news/2006/10/16/TopStories/Unholster.The.Colt-2351553.shtml?norewrite200610162157&sourcedomain=www.dailytexanonline.com|title=Unholster the Colt, Longhorns beat early deficit to take 63–31 win over Baylor|author1=Wilkerson, William  |author2=Killian, Ryan|date=October 16, 2006|newspaper=[[The Daily Texan]]|location=University of Texas at Austin|access-date=October 16, 2006}}{{dead link|date=June 2016|bot=medic}}{{cbignore|bot=medic}}</ref>  Bell then ran down the field flashing the upside-down [[Hook 'em Horns]] sign.<ref name="unhloster"/>  Baylor added a field goal to outscore Texas 10 – 0 in the first quarter.<ref name="No 6 Texas">{{cite web|url=http://www.mackbrown-texasfootball.com/index.php?s=&url_channel_id=40&url_article_id=2500&url_subchannel_id=&change_well_id=2 |title=No. 6 Texas 63, Baylor 31 |publisher=University of Texas at Austin Department of Athletics |agency=[[Associated Press]] |author=Vertuno, Jim |date=October 14, 2006 |access-date=October 16, 2006 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20061024121716/http://www.mackbrown-texasfootball.com/index.php?s=&url_channel_id=40&url_article_id=2500&url_subchannel_id=&change_well_id=2 |archive-date=October 24, 2006 |url-status=dead |df=mdy }}</ref>  Texas forced 3 turnovers in the second quarter, scoring touchdowns off each of them.<ref name="No 6 Texas"/>  Texas scored a fourth touchdown to take a 28 – 10 lead into half time. The two teams each scored 14 points in the third quarter while in the final quarter Texas scored 21 to 7 points by Baylor. The final score was Texas 63, Baylor 31.<ref name="No 6 Texas"/>

Texas quarterback Colt McCoy threw six touchdown passes in the game, the most ever by a Texas quarterback.<ref name="No 6 Texas"/>  The previous record of 5 touchdown passes had been held by [[James Brown (quarterback)|James Brown]] (set vs Baylor in 1994) and [[Chris Simms]] (vs Oklahoma State in 2001).<ref name="unhloster"/><ref name="No 6 Texas"/>  McCoy completed 21-of-32 attempted passes for a total of {{convert|275|yd|m}}, with one interception.<ref name="unhloster"/>  The game moved McCoy from eighth to fifth in the list of quarterbacks by passing efficiency, and the 18 touchdown passes were the third most in the nation.<ref name="smote"/> Following the game, McCoy was selected as the [[Cingular All-America Player of the Week]].<ref name="Cingular">{{cite web|url=http://www.mackbrown-texasfootball.com/index.php?s=&url_channel_id=40&url_article_id=2527&url_subchannel_id=&change_well_id=2 |title=McCoy named Cingular All-America Player of the Week |publisher=University of Texas at Austin Department of Athletics |date=October 19, 2006 |access-date=October 20, 2006 |url-status=dead |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20061028014457/http://www.mackbrown-texasfootball.com/index.php?s=&url_channel_id=40&url_subchannel_id=&url_article_id=2527&change_well_id=2 |archive-date=October 28, 2006 }}</ref>
