The Longhorns come into the game at Texas Tech with several injuries on defense. Five&nbsp;days prior to the game, the Longhorns announced that starting defensive tackle Derek Lokey, injured in the Nebraska game, was out indefinitely with a broken left leg.<ref name="injuries pile-up"/>  Senior defensive end Tim Crowder said. "That's huge. He's the heart and soul of our defensive line. He didn't get a lot of credit, but he was the anchor."<ref name="injuries pile-up"/>  Lokey had 24 tackles and quarterback pressures and also spent some time at fullback.<ref name="injuries pile-up"/>  Luke Tiemann will take over Lokey's fullback duties while sophomore Roy Miller will start at Lokey's spot as nose tackle.<ref name="injuries pile-up"/>  Roddrick Muckelroy, who started the first three games at linebacker, was still out indefinitely due to a ruptured finger tendon.<ref name="injuries pile-up"/>  Linebacker [[Robert Killebrew]] was listed questionable for the Texas Tech game.<ref name="injuries pile-up"/>  In addition, several defensive players had been playing with injuries. According to the ''[[Houston Chronicle]]'', these include twin-brother safeties "Marcus Griffin (ankle) and Michael Griffin (ankle), tackle Frank Okam (knee), cornerbacks Tarell Brown (toe) and Aaron Ross (hand) and end Brian Robison (ankle)". Linebacker Drew Kelson has also been suffering from an ankle injury.<ref name="injuries pile-up"/>  The 2005&nbsp;national championship team only had one game missed by a defensive starter due to injury, but the 2006&nbsp;team already had thirteen missed games due to injuries or suspensions.<ref name="injuries pile-up"/>

During Mack Brown's time at Texas, he has only lost six&nbsp;road games in an opponent's stadium.<ref name = "is it end"/>  The Red Raiders are the only team so far to have defeated the Longhorns twice in road games, and they have done so in four-year increments with victories in 1998 and 2002.<ref name="is it end">{{cite news |url=http://www.statesman.com/sports/content/sports/stories/longhorns/10/27/27texfoot.html|title=Is it end of road for UT's streak? – History tells us that the Longhorns may be due for a loss in Lubbock|newspaper=[[Austin American-Statesman]]|author=Halliburton, Suzanne|date=October 27, 2006|access-date=October 31, 2006|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20061109190242/http://www.statesman.com/sports/content/sports/stories/longhorns/10/27/27texfoot.html|archive-date=November 9, 2006}}</ref><ref name="Texas-sized comeback"/>  The 2002 game was lost by Texas 42–38, when defensive starters [[Derrick Johnson]], Kalen Thornton, [[Marcus Tubbs]] and [[Nathan Vasher]] were all injured.<ref name="injuries pile-up"/>  Texas' coaching staff said that injuries are not an excuse to lose a ball game.<ref name="Injurites test">{{cite news |url=http://www.dailytexanonline.com/media/storage/paper410/news/2006/10/25/Sports/Injuries.Testing.Depth.Of.Texas.Defense-2399935-page2.shtml?norewrite200610251226&sourcedomain=www.dailytexanonline.com|title=Injurites testing depth of Texas defense|newspaper=[[The Daily Texan]]|location=University of Texas at Austin|author=Wilkerson, William|date=October 25, 2006|access-date=October 31, 2006}}{{dead link|date=June 2016|bot=medic}}{{cbignore|bot=medic}}</ref>  Mack Brown said, "It's still a loss. Nobody cares. I used to talk about injuries all the time, and it does nothing good for your team. Some years you have them, and some years you don't... We've still got a chance for everything after eight games, and that's unbelievable."<ref name="injuries pile-up">{{cite news |url=http://www.chron.com/disp/story.mpl/sports/college/4282410.html|title=Injuries mount at Texas – Lokey is latest defensive player to be sidelined|newspaper=[[Houston Chronicle]]|author=Duarte, Joseph|date=October 23, 2006|access-date=October 24, 2006}}</ref>  The most recent two Tech-Texas games had not been close score-wise, with Texas winning 51–21 in Lubbock in 2004 and 52–17 in Austin in 2005.<ref name="winless career">{{cite news|url=http://www.lubbockonline.com/stories/102806/col_102806009.shtml|title=Texas Tech seniors try to avoid winless career vs. Longhorns|newspaper=[[Lubbock Avalanche-Journal]]|author=Williams, Don|date=October 28, 2006|access-date=October 31, 2006|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20070927205716/http://www.lubbockonline.com/stories/102806/col_102806009.shtml|archive-date=September 27, 2007|url-status=dead|df=mdy-all}}</ref>

Texas Tech entered the game with a potent passing offense led by Sophomore quarterback [[Graham Harrell]]. He has completed 245 passes out of 356 attempts this year and he averages more than 313 passing yards per game.<ref name="Tech test">{{cite news |url=http://www.dailytexanonline.com/media/storage/paper410/news/2006/10/27/Sports/Tech-Will.Test.Longhorn.Secondary-2406219.shtml?norewrite200610271244&sourcedomain=www.dailytexanonline.com|title=Tech will test secondary|newspaper=[[The Daily Texan]]|location=University of Texas at Austin|author=Killian, Ryan|date=October 23, 2006|access-date=October 31, 2006}}{{dead link|date=June 2016|bot=medic}}{{cbignore|bot=medic}}</ref>  Just the previous week, he threw for {{convert|368|yd|m}} and career -high six touchdowns in a 42–26 win over Iowa State and the Red Raiders are averaging 48.6 passes per game.<ref name="Red hand">{{cite news |url=http://www.statesman.com/sports/content/sports/stories/longhorns/10/23/23golden.html|title=Red Raiders won't hand Horns win|newspaper=[[Austin American-Statesman]]|author=Golden, Cedric|date=October 23, 2006|access-date=October 31, 2006|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20061107175110/http://www.statesman.com/sports/content/sports/stories/longhorns/10/23/23golden.html|archive-date=November 7, 2006}}</ref><ref name="Hub city scare">{{cite news |url=http://www.dailytoreador.com/media/storage/paper870/news/2006/10/27/Sports/Hub-City.Scare.Tech.Looks.To.Up.Upend.FifthRanked.horns-2405481.shtml?norewrite200610271453&sourcedomain=www.dailytoreador.com&mkey=2350641|title=Hub City scare: Tech looks to up upend fifth-ranked 'Horns|newspaper=[[The Daily Toreador]]|location=Texas Tech University|author=Cram, Travis|date=October 27, 2006|access-date=October 31, 2006}}{{dead link|date=June 2016|bot=medic}}{{cbignore|bot=medic}}</ref>

On the other side of the ball, the Longhorns were ranked 84th in pass defense this season, giving up {{convert|221.8|yd|m}} per game.<ref name="Injurites test"/>  Tech senior defensive lineman Chris Hudler said Texas also possessed a powerful offense, "There is no room for error", he said. "Some teams you might be able to make mistakes and make up for them later. But they (Texas) have some of the best athletes in the country."<ref name="Hub city scare"/> [[Henry Melton]], previously known as a tailback used by the Longhorns in short-yardage situations, ended up being used in the game at defensive end in an effort to give Texas more depth on defense.<ref name="he's got legs"/><ref name="Texas dives comeback">{{cite news |url=http://www.dailytexanonline.com/media/storage/paper410/news/2006/10/30/Sports/Texas.Dives.For.A.Comeback-2408462.shtml?norewrite200610301033&sourcedomain=www.dailytexanonline.com|title=Texas dives for a comeback – Sophomore Ryan Palmer breaks up Tech passes, helps stop Red Raiders|newspaper=[[The Daily Texan]]|location=University of Texas at Austin|author=Killian, Ryan|date=October 30, 2006|access-date=October 31, 2006}}{{dead link|date=June 2016|bot=medic}}{{cbignore|bot=medic}}</ref>

Four days prior to the game, the betting line in Las Vegas casinos was Texas by {{frac|11|1|2}} to 12 points.<ref name="Vegas Tech">{{cite web |url=http://www.vegas.com/gaming/sportsline_ncaaf.html|title=Sports Line|publisher=Vegas.com|access-date=October 24, 2006|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20061016033124/http://www.vegas.com/gaming/sportsline_ncaaf.html|archive-date=October 16, 2006|url-status=live}}</ref>  The game set a new attendance record for Texas Tech in [[Lubbock, Texas|Lubbock]], with 56,158 in attendance.<ref name="lubbockonline"/>

Texas Tech got off to a quick start, outscoring the Longhorns 21–0 in the first quarter.<ref name="Texas avoids">{{cite news |url=http://www.dailytexanonline.com/media/storage/paper410/news/2006/10/30/TopStories/Texas.Avoids.Lubbock.Letdown-2408524.shtml?norewrite200610301032&sourcedomain=www.dailytexanonline.com|title=Texas avoids Lubbock letdown – Longhorns come back from 21-point deficit to defeat Texas Tech|newspaper=[[The Daily Texan]]|location=University of Texas at Austin|author=Wilkerson, William|date=October 23, 2006|access-date=October 31, 2006}}{{dead link|date=June 2016|bot=medic}}{{cbignore|bot=medic}}</ref>  Harrell compiled 364 passing yards and three touchdown passes against the Longhorn pass defense during the first half. That was more yards passing than Texas had previously given up during an entire game in the course of the 2006 season to date.<ref name="Texas-sized comeback"/>  Texas responded by outscoring Texas Tech 21–10 in the second quarter so that Tech led by 10 points at half-time.<ref name="Longhorns rally at Tech"/>

By the end of the game, Texas Tech had completed {{convert|519|yd|m}} passing.<ref name="Texas avoids"/>  This was only {{convert|14|yd|m}} short of the record for most-ever passing yardage against the Longhorns which was set by David Klinger of the University of Houston in 1992.<ref name="Texas-sized comeback"/><ref name="Leader of Men"/>  Nevertheless, Tech found themselves trailing 35–31 with 4:24 left in the game and facing fourth down at the Texas {{convert|15|yd|m|adj=on}} line.<ref name="Texas avoids"/> According to UT's Brian Robison, "We recognized from the [offensive lines] splits that it was going to be a quarterback sneak,"<ref name="Texas avoids"/>  Texas stopped Graham Harrell to take possession of the football. Texas in turn was facing a third down and five yards-to-go with one and a half minutes left in the game.<ref name="Colt 35">{{cite news |url=http://www.dailytoreador.com/media/storage/paper870/news/2006/10/30/Sports/Colt-35-2407927.shtml?norewrite200610311133&sourcedomain=www.dailytoreador.com|title=Colt 35 – Longhorns erase 21–0 deficit to upend Raiders|newspaper=[[The Daily Toreador]]|location=Texas Tech University|author=Cram, Travis|date=October 23, 2006|access-date=October 31, 2006}}{{dead link|date=June 2016|bot=medic}}{{cbignore|bot=medic}}</ref>  McCoy dropped back to pass but was under pressure from the defense. He first ran right and then revered direction to break into the open for a {{convert|34|yd|m|adj=on}} gain before sliding down in the field of play.<ref name="Colt 35"/>

Playing with a busted lip since early in the game, Colt McCoy ended up with 256 passing yards and four touchdowns.<ref name="Texas-sized comeback"/><ref name="Leader of Men"/><ref name="Mike Finger name"/>  He also contributed career-best 68 rushing yards, including the {{convert|33|yd|m|adj=on}} scramble that helped seal the victory.<ref name="he's got legs"/><ref name="Mike Finger name"/> The win may have had added significance for McCoy since he grew up only two hours from Lubbock, attended Tuscola Jim Ned high school, and is dating a Texas Tech student who serves as a recruiter for the Red Raiders football team.<ref name="Leader of Men"/><ref name="earns shuns">{{cite news |url=http://www.dailytexanonline.com/media/storage/paper410/news/2006/10/30/Sports/Mccoy.Earns.Shuns.Attention.After.Dramatic.Wins-2408470.shtml?norewrite200610311455&sourcedomain=www.dailytexanonline.com|title=McCoy earns, shuns attention after dramatic wins|newspaper=[[The Daily Texan]]|location=University of Texas at Austin|author=Wilkerson, William|date=October 30, 2006|access-date=October 31, 2006}}{{dead link|date=June 2016|bot=medic}}{{cbignore|bot=medic}}</ref>  Although McCoy has now completed 24 touchdowns (2 shy of the UT single season record) he is known for shunning the limelight, preferring to give credit to his coaches and teammates.<ref name="earns shuns"/> Former Texas wide receiver [[Roy Williams (wide receiver)|Roy Williams]], who attended the game on the sidelines, said of McCoy "And you know what the scary thing is?  He's not even good yet."<ref name="Mike Finger name">{{cite news |url=http://www.mysanantonio.com/sports/big12/stories/MYSA102906.14C.COL.FBCfinger.horns.366c3a5.html|title=McCoy making name of his own|newspaper=[[San Antonio Express-News]]|author=Finger, Mike|date=October 29, 2006|access-date=October 31, 2006|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20070929104832/http://www.mysanantonio.com/sports/big12/stories/MYSA102906.14C.COL.FBCfinger.horns.366c3a5.html|archive-date=September 29, 2007|url-status=dead}}</ref>

For Texas, coming back to win the game from a 21–0 deficit was the second biggest comeback win in school history and the biggest comeback ever in a road game.<ref name="Leader of Men"/><ref name="21 point difference">{{cite news |url=http://www.dailytoreador.com/news/2006/10/30/|title=Texas' second-half defense makes up 21-point difference|newspaper=[[The Daily Toreador]]|location=Texas Tech University|author=Langley, Jeff|date=October 30, 2006|access-date=October 30, 2006|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20070929084145/http://www.dailytoreador.com/news/2006/10/30/|archive-date=September 29, 2007|url-status=dead}}</ref>  The Longhorns over came four turn-overs they committed during the win.<ref name="Texas-sized comeback">{{cite magazine |url=http://sportsillustrated.cnn.com/2006/football/ncaa/10/29/texas.texastech.ap/index.html|title=Texas-sized comeback – McCoy rallies Longhorns from 21 down vs. Texas Tech|magazine=[[Sports Illustrated]]|date=October 29, 2006|access-date=October 31, 2006|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20061101040449/http://sportsillustrated.cnn.com/2006/football/ncaa/10/29/texas.texastech.ap/index.html|archive-date=November 1, 2006}}</ref>  For Texas Tech, the loss was only the third loss under Coach Leach when the Raiders held a lead at half-time.<ref name="Longhorns rally at Tech">{{cite news |url=http://www.mysanantonio.com/sports/big12/stories/MYSA102906.01C.FBChorns.tech.gamer.366c209.html|title=Football: Longhorns rally for victory in Lubbock against upset-minded Texas Tech|newspaper=[[San Antonio Express-News]]|author=McDonald, Jeff|date=October 29, 2006|access-date=October 31, 2006|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20061108060028/http://www.mysanantonio.com/sports/big12/stories/MYSA102906.01C.FBChorns.tech.gamer.366c209.html|archive-date=November 8, 2006|url-status=dead}}</ref>  The {{convert|519|yd|m}} passing by Graham Harrell was a career-best.<ref name="potent misfires"/> Wide receiver Jarrett Hicks had nine catches and the most receiving yards in the game at 156, but he left the game with an apparent head injury early in the second half.<ref name="potent misfires">{{cite news |url=http://www.mysanantonio.com/sports/big12/stories/MYSA102906.14C.FBCtech.offense.366c405.html|title=Football: Potent Texas Tech offense misfires when needed most|newspaper=[[San Antonio Express-News]]|author=Griffin, Tim|date=October 29, 2006|access-date=October 31, 2006|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20070929083636/http://www.mysanantonio.com/sports/big12/stories/MYSA102906.14C.FBCtech.offense.366c405.html|archive-date=September 29, 2007|url-status=dead}}</ref>  After the game, the ''[[Lubbock Avalanche-Journal]]'' interviewed fans and reported "Many agreed that it was the most exciting game of the year, citing the big crowd and enthusiasm in the stadium."<ref name="excitement">{{cite news|url=http://www.lubbockonline.com/stories/103006/col_103006015.shtml|title=Excitement grips crowd at Tech-Texas game|newspaper=[[Lubbock Avalanche-Journal]]|author=Briscoe, Robin|date=October 29, 2006|access-date=October 30, 2006|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20070927210024/http://www.lubbockonline.com/stories/103006/col_103006015.shtml|archive-date=September 27, 2007|url-status=dead|df=mdy-all}}</ref>

After one pass interference penalty against the Red Raider, Tech defensive back Darcel McBath threw his mouthpiece towards the officials.<ref name="he's got legs">{{cite news |url=http://www.mysanantonio.com/sports/big12/stories/MYSA102906.EN.FBChorns.notebook.85011c63.html|title=Texas-Texas Tech notebook: UT's McCoy has legs & and he knows how to use them|newspaper=[[San Antonio Express-News]]|date=October 29, 2006|access-date=October 31, 2006|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20061108055754/http://www.mysanantonio.com/sports/big12/stories/MYSA102906.EN.FBChorns.notebook.85011c63.html|archive-date=November 8, 2006|url-status=dead}}</ref> After the game, Texas Tech coach [[Mike Leach (American football coach)|Mike Leach]] implied that there may have been mistakes made by the officiating crew.<ref name="thinly veiled">{{cite news|url=http://www.lubbockonline.com/stories/103006/col_103006015.shtml|title=Leach might ask Big 12 to explain questionable calls|newspaper=[[Lubbock Avalanche-Journal]]|author=Williams, Don|date=October 30, 2006|access-date=October 31, 2006|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20070927210024/http://www.lubbockonline.com/stories/103006/col_103006015.shtml|archive-date=September 27, 2007|url-status=dead|df=mdy-all}}</ref>  He said, "There were some things that happened in the course of the game, in particular the second half, that were outside of our control that I'm not able to comment on that were very detrimental to our effort."<ref name="Colt 35"/> Leach sent Big Twelve Conference officials a video tape showing 18 calls, no calls, or other rulings that were disputed by Texas Tech.<ref name="controversial calls"/>  The disputed play-calls include plays accounting for more than half of the penalty yardage assessed against Tech as well the spot of the ball following a {{convert|14|yd|m|adj=on}} fourth down pass on Tech's penultimate drive of the game.<ref name="controversial calls"/>  The officials said that play came up less than a yard short of the first down marker, giving Texas the ball.<ref name="controversial calls">{{cite news|url=http://www.lubbockonline.com/stories/103106/col_103106012.shtml|title=Tech sends tape of controversial calls to Big 12 Conference office|newspaper=[[Lubbock Avalanche-Journal]]|author=Williams, Don|date=October 31, 2006|access-date=October 31, 2006|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20070930015502/http://www.lubbockonline.com/stories/103106/col_103106012.shtml|archive-date=September 30, 2007|url-status=dead|df=mdy-all}}</ref>
{{Clear}}

===Oklahoma State===
