With the win over Texas Tech combined with the [[2006 USC Trojans football team|USC Trojans]] losing to the [[Oregon State Beavers football|Oregon State Beavers]],<ref name="thwarts">{{cite web|url=http://msn.foxsports.com/cfb/story/6115120 |title=Oregon State thwarts USC comeback |work=[[Fox Sports (USA)|Fox Sports]] |author=Petterson, Anne M. |date=October 28, 2006 |access-date=October 29, 2006 }}{{dead link|date=June 2017 |bot=InternetArchiveBot |fix-attempted=yes }}</ref> Texas moved up one spot to No. 4 in the Associated Press and Coach's polls.<ref name="AP-10-29">{{cite web |url=http://msn.foxsports.com/cfb/poll?poll=AP |title=AP Poll |work=[[Fox Sports (USA)|Fox Sports]] |access-date=October 29, 2006 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20061205222739/http://msn.foxsports.com/cfb/poll?poll=AP |archive-date=December 5, 2006 |url-status=dead }}</ref>  According to [[Fox College Sports|Fox Sports]] columnist [[Eric Moneypenny]], "...we're gonna be left with Ohio State or Michigan against Texas. Maybe West Virginia can stay the course and somehow stay above Texas in the standings, but it seems doubtful should Texas run the table and win the Big 12."<ref name="USC loss">{{cite web|title=USC's loss throws title into tizzy|url=http://msn.foxsports.com/cfb/story/6115082|work=[[Fox Sports (USA)|Fox Sports]]|author=Moneypenny, Eric|date=October 29, 2006|access-date=October 29, 2006|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20061109194921/http://msn.foxsports.com/cfb/story/6115082|archive-date=November 9, 2006|url-status=dead|df=mdy-all}}</ref>  Texas got no boost in the BCS rankings however, as they passed USC but were passed by idle [[Louisville Cardinals|Louisville]] to remain at number seven in the BCS.<ref name="BCS Oct 29">{{cite magazine |url=http://sportsillustrated.cnn.com/2006/football/ncaa/10/29/bcs.standings.ap/index.html|title=Third Watch – West Virginia moves to No. 3 in latest BCS standings|magazine=[[Sports Illustrated]]|date=October 29, 2006|access-date=October 31, 2006|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20061102112736/http://sportsillustrated.cnn.com/2006/football/ncaa/10/29/bcs.standings.ap/index.html|archive-date=November 2, 2006}}</ref>

In each of the preceding four games, Texas trailed their opponent for at least part of the game and managed to come back to win. This includes fourth quarter come-backs against Nebraska and Texas Tech. The win over Texas Tech was the biggest road come-back in UT's history, and the second biggest come-back ever.<ref name="Leader of Men"/>  The biggest was against the [[Oklahoma State University|Oklahoma State Cowboys]] in 2004 in Austin.<ref name="Leader of Men">{{cite web |url=https://www.espn.com/college-football/gamedayFinal?page=gamedayFinal9|title=Leader of Men|publisher=[[ESPN]]|author=Griffin, Tim|date=October 29, 2006|access-date=October 31, 2006}}</ref>

The week prior to the 2006 game against Texas the Cowboys had scored 28 unanswered points in a come-from-behind upset of the Nebraska Cornhuskers.<ref name="things we learned">{{cite web |url=https://www.espn.com/college-football/gamedayFinal?page=gamedayFinal9|title=Five Things We Learned This Weekend – 5. That reports of Nebraska's return to prominence were also premature|publisher=[[ESPN]]|author=Mandell, Stewart|date=October 29, 2006|access-date=October 31, 2006}}</ref>
{{Clear}}

===Kansas State===
[[File:KSU on offense at Texas 2006 cropped.jpg|right|thumb|Josh Freeman uses all of his 6'6 frame to pass over the Texas line.]]
{{Linescore Amfootball|
|Road=Texas
|R1=7
