This game marked the 113th meeting between Texas and the [[Texas A&M Aggies]] in college football and the game is part of a multi-sport rivalry called the [[Texas–Texas A&M football rivalry|Lone Star Showdown]]. It is the Longhorns' longest-running rivalry and Texas led the series, 73–34–5,<ref name="aggie game week">{{cite web |url=http://www.mackbrown-texasfootball.com/index.php?s=&url_channel_id=47&url_article_id=2300&change_well_id=2|title=Longhorns gameweek: Texas A&M|publisher=University of Texas at Austin Department of Athletics|date=November 23, 2006|access-date=November 23, 2006|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20061101023224/http://www.mackbrown-texasfootball.com/index.php?s=&url_channel_id=47&url_article_id=2300&change_well_id=2|archive-date=November 1, 2006|url-status=live}}</ref> including the last six in a row.<ref name="six in a row">{{cite news|url=http://www.thebatt.com/media/storage/paper657/news/2006/11/22/Sports/Chadvantage-2506576.shtml?norewrite200611220920&sourcedomain=www.thebatt.com |archive-url=https://archive.today/20130105000453/http://www.thebatt.com/media/storage/paper657/news/2006/11/22/Sports/Chadvantage-2506576.shtml?norewrite200611220920&sourcedomain=www.thebatt.com |url-status=dead |archive-date=January 5, 2013 |title=Can the Aggies snap their six-game losing streak against Texas? |author1=Pederson, Eric |author2=Ransom, Eric |newspaper=[[The Battalion]] |location=Texas A&M University |date=November 22, 2006 |access-date=November 23, 2006 }}</ref>  During the week before the game, the Longhorns conducted their traditional [[Hex Rally]]<ref name="Hex1">{{cite news |url=http://media.www.dailytexanonline.com/media/storage/paper410/news/2006/11/21/TopStories/Dont-Mess.With.Hexes-2505505.shtml?sourcedomain=www.dailytexanonline.com&MIIHost=media.collegepublisher.com|title=Don't mess with hexes|newspaper=[[The Daily Texan]]|location=University of Texas at Austin|author=West, Michelle|date=November 21, 2006|access-date=November 23, 2006|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20070930221501/http://media.www.dailytexanonline.com/media/storage/paper410/news/2006/11/21/TopStories/Dont-Mess.With.Hexes-2505505.shtml?sourcedomain=www.dailytexanonline.com&MIIHost=media.collegepublisher.com|archive-date=September 30, 2007|url-status=dead}}</ref> while the Aggies had an off-campus version of their traditional [[Aggie Bonfire|Bonfire]]<ref name="Bonfire1">{{cite news |url=http://www.dailytexanonline.com/media/storage/paper410/news/2006/11/21/University/Am.Bonfire.Changes.Location-2505426.shtml?norewrite200611211713&sourcedomain=www.dailytexanonline.com|title=Bonfire Changes Location|newspaper=[[The Daily Texan]]|location=University of Texas at Austin|date=November 21, 2006|access-date=November 21, 2006}}{{dead link|date=June 2016|bot=medic}}{{cbignore|bot=medic}}</ref><ref>{{cite web |url=http://www.studentbonfire.com/pages/about.html|title=About student bonfire|publisher=Student Bonfire|access-date=March 31, 2007|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20070315203742/http://www.studentbonfire.com/pages/about.html|archive-date=March 15, 2007}}</ref><ref name="Bonfire2">{{cite news|url=http://www.thebatt.com/media/storage/paper657/news/2006/11/22/News/Btho-T.u-2506607.shtml?norewrite200611230829&sourcedomain=www.thebatt.com |archive-url=https://archive.today/20130203173005/http://www.thebatt.com/media/storage/paper657/news/2006/11/22/News/Btho-T.u-2506607.shtml?norewrite200611230829&sourcedomain=www.thebatt.com |url-status=dead |archive-date=February 3, 2013 |title=BTHO t.u! |author=Selvidge, Spencer |newspaper=[[The Battalion]] |location=Texas A&M University |date=November 22, 2006 |access-date=November 23, 2006 }}</ref> and also staged a parade just prior to the game.<ref name="aggie parade">{{cite web |url=http://dmc-news.tamu.edu/templates/?a=3749&z=15|title=A&M News|access-date=November 23, 2006}} {{dead link|date=July 2011}}</ref> The Longhorns announced that starting quarterback Colt McCoy, who was injured in the game against Kansas State, was cleared to play the game against the Aggies.<ref name="McCoy cleared">{{cite news |url=http://www.dailytexanonline.com/media/storage/paper410/news/2006/11/21/Sports/Mccoy.Set.To.Play.Against.Aggies-2505461-page2.shtml?norewrite200611211716&sourcedomain=www.dailytexanonline.com|title=McCoy set to play against Aggies|author=Killian, Ryan|newspaper=[[The Daily Texan]]|location=University of Texas at Austin|date=November 21, 2006|access-date=November 23, 2006}}{{dead link|date=June 2016|bot=medic}}{{cbignore|bot=medic}}</ref>

[[File:Stephen McGee.jpg|left|thumb|Texas tackles Stephen McGee.]]
Since the series began in 1894<ref name="all time results"/> it has traditionally been played on [[Thanksgiving Day]] or Thanksgiving weekend.<ref name="atm game notes">{{cite web|url=http://www.mackbrown-texasfootball.com/doc_lib/2006_atm_notes.pdf |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20080414091143/http://www.mackbrown-texasfootball.com/doc_lib/2006_atm_notes.pdf |url-status=dead |archive-date=April 14, 2008 |title=Game Notes – Texas A&M |publisher=University of Texas at Austin Department of Athletics |access-date=November 23, 2006 }}</ref> The 2006 game marked the thirteenth straight game to be scheduled the day after Thanksgiving.<ref name="atm game notes"/> On Thanksgiving Day (one day prior to the game), the sports line in Vegas casinos had Texas favored by 13 to {{frac|13|1|2}} points<ref name="aggie vegas">{{cite web |url=http://www.vegas.com/gaming/sportsline_ncaaf.html|title=Sports Line NCAA football|access-date=November 23, 2006|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20061206203959/http://www.vegas.com/gaming/sportsline_ncaaf.html|archive-date=December 6, 2006|url-status=live}}</ref> while the weather forecast called for mostly sunny skies, a high near {{convert|81|°F|°C}}, and winds up to {{convert|15|mi/h|km/h}} from the South.<ref name="aggie weather">{{cite web |url=http://www.dailytexanonline.com/home/weather/|title=Austin Weather|newspaper=[[The Daily Texan]]|location=University of Texas at Austin|access-date=November 23, 2006|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20061026171817/http://www.dailytexanonline.com/home/weather/|archive-date=October 26, 2006}}</ref>  The 2006 meeting was the first time in eight years that both teams entered the game with at least eight wins.<ref name="eight games">{{cite news |url=http://www.statesman.com/sports/content/sports/stories/longhorns/11/20/20texfoot.html|title=Aggies and Longhorns need Friday's game to rid themselves of November melancholy|newspaper=[[Austin American-Statesman]]|author=Halliburton, Suzanne|date=November 20, 2006|access-date=November 23, 2006}}{{dead link|date=June 2016|bot=medic}}{{cbignore|bot=medic}}</ref>  Going back to 1999 when UT lost the final three games of the season, the Longhorns had gone 87 games without losing back-to-back games.<ref name="atm game notes"/>  That was the longest active streak for any college or professional football team.<ref name="atm game notes"/>

During 2006, the Texas A&M [[defensive coordinator]] was [[Gary Darnell]] who had been fired as defensive coordinator at the University of Texas under [[John Mackovic]].<ref name="Darnell">{{cite news |url=http://www.statesman.com/sports/content/sports/stories/longhorns/11/25/25aggies2.html|title=For Darnell and Aggies defense, redepmtion is sweet|date=November 24, 2006|access-date=November 25, 2006}}{{dead link|date=June 2016|bot=medic}}{{cbignore|bot=medic}}</ref>

For the third time during the season, the pre-game activities consisted of a military [[Flypast|flyby]].<ref name="atm game notes"/> Unlike the first two, which featured jets, this one was performed by [[AH-1 Cobra]] helicopters from the Marine Light Attack Helicopter Squadron HMLA-773 from [[Atlanta]].<ref name="atm game notes"/> Texas A&M won the coin toss and deferred, Texas elected to receive, and Texas A&M chose to defend the south end-zone.<ref name="Texas a&m game notes"/>  Kick-off was delayed while ABC side-line reporter [[Stacey Dales]] did her pre-game report from the north end-zone.<ref name="aggie game notebook"/> Soccer star [[Mia Hamm]] was spotted in the audience rooting for Texas.<ref name = "aggie game notebook"/>

Texas's first drive of the game ended when Henry Melton was stopped on a fourth-and-one run at the Aggie eight-yard (7&nbsp;m) line.<ref name="aggie box score">{{cite web|url=http://www.mackbrown-texasfootball.com/000_stats/06/ut12.htm |title=Texas A&M vs Texas (Nov 24, 2006) |date=November 24, 2006 |publisher=University of Texas at Austin Department of Athletics |access-date=November 25, 2006 |url-status=dead |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20071013155554/http://www.mackbrown-texasfootball.com/000_stats/06/ut12.htm |archive-date=October 13, 2007 |df=mdy }}</ref>  The Aggies drove the ball the length of the field to score a touchdown, but missed on the extra point to leave the game at 6–0.<ref name="aggie box score"/>

With about four minutes to play in the first half, McCoy connected with Limas Sweed for an apparent five-yard (5&nbsp;m) touchdown pass.<ref name="aggie box score"/><ref name="Excuses are no good">{{cite news |url=http://www.statesman.com/sports/content/sports/stories/longhorns/11/25/25golden.html|title=Excuses are no good – If Longhorns had scored more points, bad call in A&M loss wouldn't matter|newspaper=[[Austin American-Statesman]]|author=Golden, Cedric|date=November 25, 2006|access-date=November 25, 2006}}{{dead link|date=June 2016|bot=medic}}{{cbignore|bot=medic}}</ref>  However, Sweed was flagged for offensive pass interference which the Austin American-Statesman later suggested was "questionable".<ref name="questionable call preserves"/>  According to the Austin American-Statesman, the refs "blew the call"<ref name="Longhorns football Texas A&M">{{cite news |title=Longhorns football – Texas A&M 12, Texas 7|newspaper=[[Austin American-Statesman]]|date=November 25, 2006}}</ref> stating that replays showed that Sweed was involved in some "spirited hand checking while the ball was in the air, but neither player appeared to push or be guilty of interference".<ref name="questionable call preserves"/>  All three broadcasters working the game for ABC also initially spoke out against the penalty call, although later acknowledging that enough interference had occurred to justify the call.<ref name="Questionable call preserves"/><ref name="aggie ABC">{{cite news |title=ABC College Football – Texas A&M at Texas|work=[[American Broadcasting Company|ABC]]|date=November 24, 2006}}</ref>  In an ABC half-time interview, Mack Brown said "I thought it was a bad call," but added "There's nothing we can do about that. We should've done a better job on third down."<ref name="questionable call preserves">{{cite news|url=http://www.statesman.com/blogs/content/shared-gen/blogs/austin/longhorns/entries/2006/11/24/questionable_call_preserves_early_shutout_for_aggies.html|title=Questionable call preserves early shutout for Aggies|newspaper=[[Austin American-Statesman]]|author=Bridges, Jeff|date=November 24, 2006|access-date=November 25, 2006|url-status=dead|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20070929121124/http://www.statesman.com/blogs/content/shared-gen/blogs/austin/longhorns/entries/2006/11/24/questionable_call_preserves_early_shutout_for_aggies.html|archive-date=September 29, 2007|df=mdy-all}}</ref><ref name="aggie ABC"/>

[[File:Lone Star Showdown 2006 McGee on goal-line.jpg|left|thumb|Texas A&M takes over at their goal line after a Colt McCoy interception in the first half]]
Immediately after the penalty, Colt McCoy threw an interception near the Texas A&M goal line and the Aggies ran out the clock to take the 6–0 lead into half-time.<ref name="aggie box score"/> The zero score marked the first time the Longhorns had been held scoreless in the first half of play since the 2004 season.<ref name="Questionable call preserves">{{cite news|url=http://www.statesman.com/blogs/content/shared-gen/blogs/austin/longhorns/entries/2006/11/24/questionable_call_preserves_early_shutout_for_aggies.html|title=Questionable call preserves early shutout for Aggies|newspaper=[[Austin American-Statesman]]|author=Bridges, John|date=November 24, 2006|access-date=March 29, 2007|url-status=dead|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20070929121124/http://www.statesman.com/blogs/content/shared-gen/blogs/austin/longhorns/entries/2006/11/24/questionable_call_preserves_early_shutout_for_aggies.html|archive-date=September 29, 2007|df=mdy-all}}</ref>

The only score in the third quarter was a rushing touchdown by Jamaal Charles, his seventh of the season.<ref name="Texas a&m game notes"/><ref name="aggie box score"/>

In the fourth quarter, Texas A&M made a long-drive that took up nearly nine minutes.<ref name="defense says goodbye">{{cite news |url=http://www.statesman.com/sports/content/sports/stories/longhorns/11/25/25texside1.html|title=Longhorn run defense says goodbye to top ranking – Aggies gain 163 on two drives alone|newspaper=[[Austin American-Statesman]]|author=Rosner, Mark|date=November 24, 2006|access-date=November 25, 2006}}{{dead link|date=June 2016|bot=medic}}{{cbignore|bot=medic}}</ref> Texas A&M quarterback [[Stephen McGee]] was playing so hard that he was throwing up during the game, but he managed to make an {{convert|8|yd|m|adj=on}} touchdown run with 2:32 left to play.<ref name="snaps six streak">{{cite web |url=http://sports.espn.go.com/ncf/recap?gameId=263280251|archive-url=https://archive.today/20121208134545/http://sports.espn.go.com/ncf/recap?gameId=263280251|url-status=dead|archive-date=December 8, 2012|title=Texas A&M runs down Texas to snap 6-game series skid|publisher=[[ESPN]]|agency=[[Associated Press]]|date=November 24, 2006|access-date=November 25, 2006}}</ref>  A&M was unable to convert their two-point attempt after the touchdown.<ref name="snaps six streak"/>

With 1:21&nbsp;seconds left in the game and Texas trailing 12–7, McCoy threw his third interception of the game.<ref name="aggie box score"/>  Following the change of possession and sometime near the whistle signalling the end of the play on the field, Aggie Kellen Heard knocked McCoy to the ground as McCoy walked along the field unbuckling his chin strap. Heard was ejected from the game as a result of the "cheap-shot late hit"<ref name="Longhorns football Texas A&M"/><ref name="turn out lights">{{cite news |url=http://blogs.chron.com/sportsjustice/archives/2006/11/acres_of_empty.html|title=Turn out the lights, Longhorn fans, the party's over|newspaper=[[Houston Chronicle]]|author=Justice, Richard|date=November 24, 2006|access-date=November 25, 2006|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20061126182617/http://blogs.chron.com/sportsjustice/archives/2006/11/acres_of_empty.html|archive-date=November 26, 2006|url-status=dead}}</ref><ref name="two leave">{{cite news |url=http://www.dallasnews.com/s/dws/nwsltr/sports/ut/stories/120106dnspoutnewsletter.2b09ace.html|title=Two leave before UT knows where it's going|newspaper=[[The Dallas Morning News]]|date=December 7, 2006|access-date=March 29, 2007|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20070205150449/http://www.dallasnews.com/s/dws/nwsltr/sports/ut/stories/120106dnspoutnewsletter.2b09ace.html|archive-date=February 5, 2007|url-status=dead}}</ref> and the Aggies were penalized {{convert|15|yd|m|spell=in}}.<ref name="aggie box score"/>  McCoy was later quoted as saying that the tackle had not been a cheap shot.<ref name="A&M shattered all of our dreams"/>
Texas A&M's coach [[Dennis Franchione]] later apologized for what he called a "late hit" saying "We do not teach this type of play or condone it."<ref name="Franchione apology">{{cite news |url=https://www.usatoday.com/sports/college/football/big12/2006-12-01-aggies-texas-apology_x.htm|title=Texas A&M's Franchione apologizes for late hit on McCoy|newspaper=[[USA Today]]|date=December 1, 2006|access-date=March 29, 2007}}</ref><ref>{{cite web|url=http://sportsline.com/collegefootball/story/9842281 |archive-url=https://archive.today/20120729191847/http://sportsline.com/collegefootball/story/9842281 |url-status=dead |archive-date=July 29, 2012 |title=A&M's Franchione apologizes for late hit on McCoy |work=[[CBS Sports]] |date=December 1, 2006 |access-date=March 29, 2007 }}</ref><ref>{{cite news |title=Franchione sends Longhorns apology over Heard's late hit|url=http://www.aggiesports.com/stories/120206/football_20061202051.php|newspaper=[[The Bryan-College Station Eagle]]|author=Cessna, Robert|date=December 2, 2006|access-date=March 29, 2007|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20070926232353/http://www.aggiesports.com/stories/120206/football_20061202051.php|archive-date=September 26, 2007|url-status=dead}}</ref><ref>{{cite news |url=http://media.www.dailytexanonline.com/media/storage/paper410/news/2006/12/04/Sports/Snead.Transfer.Made.Official-2519442.shtml|title=Snead transfer made official|date=December 4, 2006|author=Wilkerson, William|newspaper=[[The Daily Texan]]|location=University of Texas at Austin|access-date=March 29, 2007|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20070930222313/http://media.www.dailytexanonline.com/media/storage/paper410/news/2006/12/04/Sports/Snead.Transfer.Made.Official-2519442.shtml|archive-date=September 30, 2007|url-status=dead}}</ref>  Texas held the Aggies to three-and-out and used all three of their time-outs in order to preserve game time.<ref name="aggie box score"/>  Texas got the ball back with 48&nbsp;seconds left in the game.<ref name="aggie box score"/>

[[File:2006 Lone Star Showdown Colt McCoy injured.jpg|thumb|Colt McCoy injured]]
With 20&nbsp;seconds remaining in the game, McCoy was injured by a "vicious, stadium hushing tackle"<ref name = "what next"/> as Aggies defensive end Michael Bennett connected with his helmet under McCoy's jaw just as McCoy released a pass.<ref name = "pinched nerve 2"/><ref name="McCoy taken">{{cite news|url=http://www.statesman.com/blogs/content/shared-gen/blogs/austin/longhorns/entries/2006/11/24/mccoy_taken_to_hospital_for_observation.html|title=McCoy taken to hospital for observation|newspaper=[[Austin American-Statesman]]|date=November 24, 2006|author=Halliburton, Suzanne|access-date=March 29, 2007|url-status=dead|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20110607164811/http://www.statesman.com/blogs/content/shared-gen/blogs/austin/longhorns/entries/2006/11/24/mccoy_taken_to_hospital_for_observation.html|archive-date=June 7, 2011|df=mdy-all}}</ref> Replays shown both on television and in the stadium revealed the hit included "helmet-to-helmet"<ref name="two leave"/><ref name="Battalion01">{{cite news|url=http://media.www.thebatt.com/media/storage/paper657/news/2006/11/27/Sports/Mccoy.Lacks.Mcgees.Toughness-2508048.shtml?sourcedomain=www.thebatt.com&MIIHost=media.collegepublisher.com |title=McCoy lacks McGee's toughness |newspaper=[[The Battalion]] |location=Texas A&M University |date=November 27, 2006 |access-date=November 27, 2006 |url-status=dead |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20070929104344/http://media.www.thebatt.com/media/storage/paper657/news/2006/11/27/Sports/Mccoy.Lacks.Mcgees.Toughness-2508048.shtml?sourcedomain=www.thebatt.com&MIIHost=media.collegepublisher.com |archive-date=September 29, 2007 |df=mdy }}</ref> contact which, if intentional, is illegal in NCAA football.<ref name="NCAA rules">{{cite web |url=https://www.ncaa.org/library/rules/2006/2006_football_rules.pdf|title=2006 Football – Rules and Interpretations|publisher=National Collegiate Athletic Association|access-date=November 26, 2006|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20061119132631/http://www.ncaa.org/library/rules/2006/2006_football_rules.pdf |archive-date=November 19, 2006}}</ref> No flag was thrown on the play. When the replay was shown in the stadium, the Longhorn fans erupted in boos<ref name="snaps six streak"/> before lapsing back into silence as McCoy lay on the ground for 10&nbsp;minutes before being taken off the field on a cart.<ref name="gig is up">{{cite news |url=http://www.statesman.com/sports/content/sports/stories/longhorns/11/25/25texfoot.html|title=The gig is up – A&M shoots down Texas streaks with 12–7 win at Royal-Memorial|newspaper=[[Austin American-Statesman]]|author=Halliburton, Suzanne|date=November 25, 2006|access-date=November 26, 2006}}{{dead link|date=June 2016|bot=medic}}{{cbignore|bot=medic}}</ref>  Mack Brown said after the game "I didn't see it, but it sounded like 88,000 (fans) thought it was dirty."<ref name="snaps six streak"/><ref name="pinched nerve 2"/><ref name="McCoy taken"/>  Fellow Longhorn Selvin Young said he thought the hit was clean calling it a "textbook hit".<ref name="chin hit">{{cite news|url=http://www.dfw.com/mld/dfw/sports/colleges/16096484.htm |title=QB's day unravels in the second half |newspaper=[[Fort Worth Star-Telegram]] |author=Ransom, Eric |date=November 25, 2006 |access-date=November 26, 2006 }}{{dead link|date=June 2016|bot=medic}}{{cbignore|bot=medic}}</ref>  McCoy was taken to [[Brackenridge Hospital]] where he spent more than three hours undergoing an evaluation that included an [[X-ray]], [[MRI]], and a [[CAT scan]].<ref name = "pinched nerve 2"/><ref name="CFGD atm">{{cite video |title=SportsCenter|publisher=[[ESPN]]|date=November 26, 2006}}</ref><ref name = "pinched nerve 3"/>  Longhorns trainer Kenny Boyd said the injury was a severe pinched nerve in McCoy's neck.<ref name="pinched nerve 2"/><ref name="pinched nerve 3"/>  Boyd said that McCoy was expected to make a full recovery, but no timetable was set for McCoy to return to play.<ref name="pinched nerve 2">{{cite news |url=http://www.statesman.com/sports/content/sports/stories/longhorns/11/25/25texside2.html|title=McCoy suffers severe pinched nerve in neck – Longhorns quarterback spends three hours at hospital for testing|newspaper=[[Austin American-Statesman]]|author=Halliburton, Suzanne|date=November 25, 2006|access-date=November 26, 2006}}{{dead link|date=June 2016|bot=medic}}{{cbignore|bot=medic}}</ref><ref name="pinched nerve 3">{{cite web |url=https://www.espn.com/college-football/news/story?id=2674763|title=Texas' McCoy suffers pinched nerve, return unknown|publisher=[[ESPN]]|date=November 24, 2006|access-date=November 25, 2006}}</ref>  Colt McCoy, in a November 2007 interview, stated that neither this nor the earlier Heard tackle had been cheap shots.<ref name="A&M shattered all of our dreams">{{cite news |url=http://www.statesman.com/search/content/sports/stories/longhorns/11/15/1115texfoot.html|author=Halliburton, Suzanne|title=Colt McCoy eager to show Aggies his toughness|date=November 15, 2007|newspaper=[[Austin American-Statesman]]|access-date=November 18, 2007}}</ref>

Following the injury to McCoy, back-up quarterback Jevan Snead threw an interception on his first play of the game.<ref name="aggie box score"/> With the clock rolling on the change of possession, time expired without the Aggies needing to take a play.<ref name="aggie box score"/>

The Aggies amassed 244 rushing yards against the nation's top-ranked rushing defense.<ref name="snaps six streak"/> Texas, normally a potent running team, produced only four rushing first downs and did not have a single run longer than {{convert|9|yd|m|spell=in}}.<ref name="nowhere to run">{{cite news |url=http://www.statesman.com/sports/content/sports/stories/longhorns/11/25/25bohls.html|title=Nowhere to run for Horns – But no need to hide, either|newspaper=[[Austin American-Statesman]]|author=Bohls, Kirk|date=November 25, 2006|access-date=November 25, 2006}}{{dead link|date=June 2016|bot=medic}}{{cbignore|bot=medic}}</ref>  Jamaal Charles, in a later interview, stated "We were looking weak. We thought they'd let up because we're Texas."<ref name="A&M shattered all of our dreams"/>
The 12–7 victory was the first win for the Aggies in Austin in 12 years.<ref name="snaps six streak"/>  "They shattered all our dreams", declared Longhorn defensive end Brian Orakpo.<ref name="A&M shattered all of our dreams"/>
The game was the 40th consecutive home sell-out for the Longhorns and [[Justin Blalock]] became the first player in Texas history to start 50 consecutive game.<ref name="Texas a&m game notes"/>

